WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.680
Have you ever looked at like a perfectly designed system?

00:02.880 --> 00:05.720
And I mean, really taking the time to just look at it

00:05.720 --> 00:08.540
and assume someone or something

00:08.540 --> 00:11.860
must have sat down with a blueprint to plan the whole thing out?

00:12.240 --> 00:12.800
Oh, absolutely.

00:13.140 --> 00:13.280
Okay.

00:13.600 --> 00:14.580
I mean, we all do that, right?

00:14.580 --> 00:16.140
Right. I want you to think about this for a second

00:16.140 --> 00:17.160
if you're listening right now.

00:17.680 --> 00:20.820
Think about, you know, a brilliant complex story

00:20.820 --> 00:24.440
where every single plot thread just ties up perfectly at the very end.

00:24.480 --> 00:24.640
Yeah.

00:24.820 --> 00:27.400
Or think about a complex ecosystem.

00:27.680 --> 00:28.940
Picture a rainforest, right?

00:28.940 --> 00:32.800
Where every single insect, every fungus, every plant

00:32.800 --> 00:36.000
seems to have this specific intended role

00:36.000 --> 00:37.280
that keeps the whole thing balanced.

00:37.400 --> 00:38.860
Mm-hmm. It's like a machine.

00:39.200 --> 00:40.280
Exactly. A machine.

00:40.720 --> 00:42.080
Or even zoom out further.

00:43.120 --> 00:45.060
Think about the spiral of a galaxy

00:45.060 --> 00:48.300
just turning out there in the freezing vacuum of space.

00:48.900 --> 00:51.180
When you see that level of intricate design,

00:51.860 --> 00:55.300
the immediate, almost instinctual human reaction

00:55.300 --> 00:57.120
is to look for the designer.

00:57.660 --> 00:57.940
Yes.

00:57.940 --> 01:00.700
You just automatically assume there was a plan, right?

01:00.800 --> 01:03.080
A blueprint or an architect

01:03.080 --> 01:04.940
who envisioned the final product

01:04.940 --> 01:06.580
before the very first brick was laid.

01:06.580 --> 01:09.200
It's a deeply ingrained cognitive habit.

01:09.200 --> 01:12.540
I mean, humans are, at our core, problem-solving creatures.

01:12.800 --> 01:12.980
Right.

01:13.080 --> 01:15.400
So when we look out into the world and see a solution,

01:15.720 --> 01:19.940
we naturally assume there was a solver who, you know, intended it.

01:20.020 --> 01:21.380
We want to find the guy in charge.

01:21.600 --> 01:21.940
Totally.

01:22.440 --> 01:25.840
We look at the finished structure, this polished final product,

01:26.020 --> 01:29.780
and we project that finished state backward into the origin of the process.

01:30.220 --> 01:32.700
We just assume the end was present at the beginning.

01:32.700 --> 01:35.880
And that brings us to the real mission of our deep dive today,

01:35.880 --> 01:39.920
because we are going to fundamentally challenge that exact assumption.

01:40.260 --> 01:41.180
It's a big one to challenge.

01:41.560 --> 01:42.380
It really is.

01:42.840 --> 01:46.960
We are going to explore how incredibly complex, highly organized systems

01:46.960 --> 01:53.420
can essentially write their own rules as they go without any prior plan whatsoever.

01:53.420 --> 01:56.420
Which sounds impossible, but it happens everywhere.

01:57.000 --> 01:57.040
Right.

01:57.140 --> 02:00.040
We're going to completely strip away this illusion of the blueprint.

02:00.940 --> 02:04.960
And to do this, we're drawing on a single absolutely fascinating source today.

02:05.280 --> 02:06.260
It's such a good paper.

02:06.600 --> 02:06.940
It is.

02:07.040 --> 02:10.240
It's this independent research paper published in September 2026

02:10.240 --> 02:12.520
by a researcher named Flyxion.

02:12.840 --> 02:16.060
And the title itself is just a total mind-bender.

02:16.200 --> 02:16.320
Yeah.

02:16.460 --> 02:18.900
Histories that rewrite their own possibilities.

02:19.440 --> 02:21.060
Organization without a prior plan.

02:21.060 --> 02:26.220
Which is a mouthful, but man, it is this dense, brilliant piece of synthesis.

02:26.580 --> 02:31.820
It connects biblical literature, astrophysics, prebiotic chemistry, and philosophy.

02:32.320 --> 02:36.200
It really is a remarkable piece of work, because the core claim Flyxion makes

02:36.200 --> 02:39.980
is that the finished structure of a system does not need to exist at the beginning.

02:40.360 --> 02:41.280
Not even a little bit?

02:41.740 --> 02:42.760
Not even as information.

02:43.400 --> 02:46.840
Each stage of a process only needs to modify what the author calls

02:46.840 --> 02:50.100
the admissible continuations of the next stage.

02:50.100 --> 02:50.880
Okay, wow.

02:51.060 --> 02:54.280
But before we dive into the actual mechanics of how that works,

02:54.440 --> 02:56.700
we really need to set some ground rules for our discussion.

02:57.080 --> 03:00.140
The paper is incredibly careful about its terminology.

03:00.600 --> 03:01.180
Which is important.

03:01.680 --> 03:01.920
Very.

03:02.460 --> 03:05.920
As a listener, I want you to keep three specific definitions in mind today,

03:05.980 --> 03:08.600
because it is so, so easy to conflate them.

03:08.740 --> 03:10.000
Okay, lay them out for us.

03:10.280 --> 03:11.320
The first is order.

03:11.680 --> 03:13.820
Order just means regularity or symmetry.

03:14.000 --> 03:15.580
Think of like a crystal lattice.

03:15.580 --> 03:16.920
Okay, let's unpack this.

03:17.240 --> 03:21.700
Because when I think of a crystal, I think of something very organized.

03:22.100 --> 03:22.520
Right.

03:22.700 --> 03:25.160
It's ordered, but it's not necessarily complex.

03:25.540 --> 03:30.520
A crystal is highly ordered and highly repetitive, but it doesn't carry a lot of complex information.

03:30.520 --> 03:31.500
Oh, I see.

03:31.820 --> 03:33.520
It's just doing the same thing over and over.

03:33.840 --> 03:34.240
Exactly.

03:34.680 --> 03:36.820
It's just the same pattern repeating endlessly.

03:37.100 --> 03:40.340
Which brings us to the second term, which is complexity.

03:40.600 --> 03:40.880
Okay.

03:41.080 --> 03:44.780
Complexity means a combination of differentiation and integration.

03:45.160 --> 03:48.840
The paper uses the example of a jungle, like the rainforest you mentioned earlier.

03:48.980 --> 03:49.180
Right.

03:49.600 --> 03:51.680
A jungle is highly differentiated.

03:51.900 --> 03:55.140
You know, you have thousands of different species interacting, thousands of variables.

03:55.520 --> 03:59.740
But it lacks the rigid, repetitive predictability of a crystal.

04:00.320 --> 04:01.700
So order is a crystal.

04:02.360 --> 04:03.600
Complexity is a jungle.

04:04.360 --> 04:07.460
They are almost opposites on a spectrum, kind of.

04:07.740 --> 04:08.660
Basically, yeah.

04:09.140 --> 04:09.960
What's the third term?

04:09.960 --> 04:15.760
The third term, and the absolute primary focus of our entire discussion today, is organization.

04:16.180 --> 04:16.700
Organization.

04:17.420 --> 04:17.680
Yes.

04:18.180 --> 04:23.600
Organization is defined here as persistent differentiation into mutually constrained parts.

04:24.160 --> 04:24.480
Okay.

04:24.960 --> 04:26.600
Mutually constrained parts.

04:26.760 --> 04:28.440
That sounds a bit dense.

04:28.780 --> 04:29.740
It is a bit dense.

04:30.240 --> 04:31.080
Think of it like this.

04:31.460 --> 04:38.140
The components of the system limit and define each other, forming a cohesive structure that persists over time.

04:38.640 --> 04:38.880
Okay.

04:38.880 --> 04:39.380
Got it.

04:39.960 --> 04:45.480
The examples Phleoxin gives are, well, a galaxy with distinct components interacting gravitationally.

04:45.760 --> 04:48.780
Or a living cell with organelles that rely on each other.

04:49.340 --> 04:49.740
Right.

04:49.900 --> 04:51.300
They need each other to work.

04:52.020 --> 04:52.380
Exactly.

04:52.540 --> 04:56.120
Or even a narrative that successfully delivers its information to an audience.

04:56.480 --> 04:58.600
The parts are constrained by the whole.

04:58.760 --> 05:00.060
That makes sense.

05:00.060 --> 05:05.120
And crucially, we are talking about organization in a completely neutral sense here.

05:05.120 --> 05:11.440
It doesn't mean the system has a, you know, a purpose or a function in the way a designed machine like a toaster does.

05:11.440 --> 05:12.040
Right.

05:12.040 --> 05:13.720
A toaster is built to make toast.

05:13.720 --> 05:16.220
A galaxy isn't built to do anything.

05:16.220 --> 05:17.840
It just does what it does.

05:17.840 --> 05:18.660
Precisely.

05:18.660 --> 05:25.260
We are purely looking at how these mutually constrained structures come to exist in the wild completely on their own.

05:25.260 --> 05:26.520
I love that.

05:27.140 --> 05:36.020
What's totally wild is that the paper starts by tackling this idea of organization in a place you might not expect for a hard scientific paper.

05:36.020 --> 05:38.100
Yeah, ancient literature.

05:38.660 --> 05:38.920
Right.

05:39.320 --> 05:44.580
Specifically, this idea of the problem of the unplanned pattern in narrative.

05:44.900 --> 05:48.720
The paper introduces what we might call the solver fallacy.

05:49.180 --> 05:53.940
As I mentioned, when we see a solved problem, we just assume an intentional solver.

05:54.100 --> 05:54.480
Of course.

05:54.840 --> 05:56.180
We see a pocket watch on a beach.

05:56.360 --> 05:57.480
We assume a watchmaker.

05:57.780 --> 06:00.540
We see a complex moral code in a society.

06:00.820 --> 06:03.420
We assume a brilliant ancient legislator.

06:03.620 --> 06:04.520
It's human nature.

06:04.520 --> 06:05.140
It is.

06:05.860 --> 06:09.820
Flyxion argues that this temptation is not irrational, though.

06:10.220 --> 06:15.000
I mean, after all, human authors do plan things, and human engineers do design things.

06:15.100 --> 06:16.660
Yeah, blueprints are a real thing.

06:16.980 --> 06:17.260
Right.

06:17.540 --> 06:26.520
But the difficulty is that the mere presence of a solved problem does not, by itself, discriminate between a conscious solver and a blind process.

06:26.960 --> 06:28.220
A blind process.

06:28.780 --> 06:33.080
A blind process that simply retained successes and discarded failures.

06:33.080 --> 06:38.240
A process that blindly retains solutions can perfectly fake a blueprint.

06:38.240 --> 06:39.200
Wow.

06:39.200 --> 06:43.340
And to illustrate this, the paper takes us deep into the book of Job in the Bible.

06:43.860 --> 06:46.400
This is the first really mind-bending example for me.

06:46.600 --> 06:47.640
It's a great example.

06:47.640 --> 06:56.740
For you listening, even if you went to Sunday school and are, like, familiar with Job, I want to walk you through exactly how this story is mechanically constructed.

06:57.100 --> 07:01.140
Because it is fascinating when you look at it as a machine.

07:01.140 --> 07:02.780
Oh, it really is a machine.

07:03.000 --> 07:09.780
The very first chapter of Job reports four massive, devastating disasters in rapid sequence.

07:09.880 --> 07:10.920
Just back to back.

07:11.060 --> 07:11.280
Right.

07:11.980 --> 07:18.880
First, a raiding party of Sabaeans swoops in, takes all of Job's oxen and donkeys, and violently slaughters all his servants.

07:19.280 --> 07:19.640
Awful.

07:19.640 --> 07:23.380
And while that guy is still speaking, another messenger runs up.

07:23.800 --> 07:27.920
Fire has literally fallen from heaven and burned up all his sheep and the shepherds.

07:27.980 --> 07:29.440
Just zero time to breathe.

07:29.740 --> 07:30.120
Exactly.

07:30.400 --> 07:33.460
And while that guy is speaking, a third messenger arrives.

07:33.880 --> 07:39.120
Another raiding party, the Chaldeans, came in three bands, seized all his camels, and killed those servants.

07:39.480 --> 07:40.500
It just keeps going.

07:40.500 --> 07:45.340
And finally, before he can even process that, a fourth messenger arrives.

07:46.000 --> 07:54.860
A great wind came off the wilderness, struck the four corners of the house where Job's children were feasting, collapsed the entire building, and killed every single one of his kids.

07:55.120 --> 08:00.520
It is an absolutely overwhelming, suffocating sequence of catastrophes.

08:00.640 --> 08:02.600
And it's designed to break a human spirit.

08:03.020 --> 08:03.180
Yeah.

08:03.180 --> 08:10.900
But what Flyxion highlights isn't the theology of the suffering, it's the exact repeated formula used to report these events.

08:11.020 --> 08:12.260
Yes, this is the crazy part.

08:12.640 --> 08:19.860
After every single one of those four disasters, a messenger arrives to Job, and the messenger says the exact same phrase,

08:20.420 --> 08:22.440
I alone have escaped to tell.

08:22.760 --> 08:23.360
Four times.

08:23.580 --> 08:24.080
Four times.

08:24.520 --> 08:27.600
In Job 1, 15, 16, 17, and 19.

08:27.760 --> 08:29.480
I alone have escaped to tell you.

08:29.480 --> 08:35.620
Which, when you stop and think about it as a factual historical event, is completely absurd.

08:36.020 --> 08:38.760
Statistically speaking, it is wildly improbable.

08:38.960 --> 08:48.580
I mean, if we read these as four independent historical events drawn from the normal space of worldly disasters, the configuration is impossibly strained.

08:48.760 --> 08:49.000
Right.

08:49.000 --> 08:51.800
You have four near-total wipeouts.

08:52.240 --> 09:01.200
In each of these chaotic, violent scenarios, armed raiders, unpredictable supernatural fire, a collapsing building, exactly one person survives.

09:01.360 --> 09:01.780
Just one.

09:02.360 --> 09:13.500
Furthermore, that single survivor manages to travel to Job and arrives at the exact right moment to deliver the news in a rapid-fire, back-to-back sequence before the next one arrives.

09:13.600 --> 09:14.200
It's ridiculous.

09:14.600 --> 09:18.720
The probability of that happening in reality is practically zero.

09:19.000 --> 09:26.240
It actually kind of reminds me of a, I was trying to think of how to explain this, but it actually reminds me of a constraint in, like, video game design.

09:26.500 --> 09:27.580
Oh, I like this.

09:27.740 --> 09:28.020
How so?

09:28.560 --> 09:29.120
Well, okay.

09:29.240 --> 09:35.900
So, if you are playing a, you know, like a massive, like, open-world video game, right?

09:35.900 --> 09:43.080
And you have to, the game's plot requires your character to learn that a nearby village is under attack.

09:43.700 --> 09:55.960
The game, the game's code and everything will force a non-player character, an NPC, to spawn, basically, like, to literally spawn into existence near you.

09:55.960 --> 09:56.560
Right.

09:56.660 --> 09:58.220
They just appear out of nowhere.

09:58.560 --> 09:58.940
Exactly.

09:59.520 --> 10:04.660
It doesn't matter how chaotic the battle in the village was or how far away you are currently standing.

10:05.080 --> 10:10.760
The game's programming requires the transmission of that specific information to you so you can go do the quest.

10:11.120 --> 10:12.860
So it generates a survivor.

10:13.380 --> 10:15.460
It generates a survivor out of Threnair.

10:15.460 --> 10:24.800
When you see that NPC running up to you screaming about the village, you don't think, wow, what a historical miracle that this specific guy survived the dragon attack.

10:25.040 --> 10:27.980
No, you realize it's a structural requirement of the software.

10:28.220 --> 10:28.520
Yes.

10:29.140 --> 10:34.840
The survivor is a constraint of the narrative machinery, not a factual puzzle to be solved.

10:35.360 --> 10:39.120
That's a really strong analogy, but let's push it a little further to fit the text here.

10:39.180 --> 10:39.320
Okay.

10:39.700 --> 10:44.020
In a video game, a conscious programmer wrote the spawn logic, right?

10:44.160 --> 10:45.120
You have an actual blueprint.

10:45.620 --> 10:45.820
Right.

10:46.220 --> 10:47.980
A developer in a studio somewhere.

10:48.220 --> 10:48.620
Exactly.

10:49.200 --> 10:56.380
But in the book of Job, the improbability of the lone survivor isn't necessarily proof of a single mastermind author.

10:56.720 --> 10:59.740
It is a structural signature of the text itself.

11:00.120 --> 11:00.720
What do you mean?

11:00.940 --> 11:08.140
The story absolutely requires that Job learns of each massive loss so he can react to it, so his faith can be tested.

11:08.560 --> 11:10.040
That's the whole point of the book.

11:10.400 --> 11:10.600
Right.

11:10.680 --> 11:12.500
If he doesn't know, there's no test.

11:12.500 --> 11:24.200
Therefore, the narrative machinery dictates that each catastrophe must be almost total to make the test severe enough, while preserving exactly one transmission channel to inform him.

11:24.700 --> 11:25.260
Ah.

11:25.260 --> 11:30.180
At the level of the text, the survivor is constrained by the narrative's need for transmission.

11:30.840 --> 11:35.420
The structure that carries the report places a requirement on the disaster itself.

11:35.420 --> 11:36.580
I see what you're saying.

11:36.940 --> 11:40.620
The apparent improbability actually exposes a generative constraint?

11:41.120 --> 11:41.900
Exactly that.

11:42.540 --> 11:50.740
Whatever historical or literary process produced the text of Job was subject to a condition that ordinary, real-world events are not subject to.

11:50.740 --> 11:51.380
Right.

11:51.500 --> 11:53.520
The real world doesn't care if you get the message.

11:54.340 --> 11:54.780
Precisely.

11:55.440 --> 12:04.400
Now, the paper is careful to note that this regularity, this lone survivor motif, reveals the constraint, but it doesn't necessarily tell us who imposed it.

12:04.580 --> 12:06.100
It could be one guy writing it.

12:06.580 --> 12:14.920
It could be a single genius author who consciously planned the whole thing out with a blueprint, but it could just as easily be a long tradition of oral storytelling.

12:15.280 --> 12:17.200
Like generations passing it down.

12:17.200 --> 12:24.980
Right. Think of generations of storytellers around a fire. Versions of the story where everyone dies are forgotten because Job never finds out.

12:25.160 --> 12:26.740
That makes a terrible story.

12:26.940 --> 12:31.240
It does. And versions where lots of people survive lessen the tragic impact.

12:31.720 --> 12:38.080
Over centuries, the story slowly converges on this perfectly efficient narrative structure through trial and error.

12:38.340 --> 12:39.180
That is so cool.

12:39.480 --> 12:41.800
The constraint is there, but the blueprint might not be.

12:42.000 --> 12:47.060
In Job, the mechanism of escape is utilized to guarantee the transmission of information.

12:47.200 --> 12:56.520
Which sets up this perfect contrast. Because if Job is a machine where escape guarantees transmission, what happens when that narrative machinery breaks down?

12:56.860 --> 12:58.160
That is the big question.

12:58.560 --> 13:01.980
Like what happens when the escape doesn't lead to a message being delivered?

13:02.480 --> 13:08.440
And that brings us to the second major literary example Flaggian explores, and it's a very famous one in biblical scholarship.

13:08.940 --> 13:10.420
The ending of the Gospel of Mark.

13:10.420 --> 13:11.800
This one blew my mind.

13:12.260 --> 13:14.420
Specifically, Mark chapter 16, verse 8.

13:14.840 --> 13:21.640
The paper references the earliest complete Greek manuscripts of Mark we have, which are the Codex Sinaiticus and the Codex Vaticanus.

13:21.840 --> 13:23.200
These are super early, right?

13:23.640 --> 13:24.440
Incredibly early.

13:24.780 --> 13:26.600
Foundational texts from the 4th century.

13:27.040 --> 13:30.820
And in these manuscripts, the Gospel ends abruptly, right at verse 8.

13:30.820 --> 13:38.540
For you listening, if you've never read the original ending of Mark, let me detail this scene because it is incredibly jarring if you aren't expecting it.

13:38.640 --> 13:39.480
It really is a shock.

13:40.080 --> 13:41.860
This is the morning of the resurrection.

13:42.500 --> 13:45.480
Mary Magdalene and the other women go to the tomb to anoint the body.

13:46.040 --> 13:48.440
They find the massive stone already rolled away.

13:49.160 --> 13:53.540
They go inside and they see a young man in a white robe who tells them Jesus has risen,

13:54.100 --> 13:58.220
and that they need to go tell his disciples and Peter that he is going ahead of them to Galilee.

13:58.220 --> 14:00.000
So they have their message.

14:00.280 --> 14:00.380
Right.

14:00.680 --> 14:01.380
They get the quest.

14:02.200 --> 14:05.640
And then, here is how the earliest manuscripts of Mark end.

14:06.340 --> 14:12.180
The women flee the empty tomb, trembling and amazed, and they say nothing to anyone out of fear.

14:13.100 --> 14:13.560
Full stop.

14:13.840 --> 14:14.240
The end.

14:14.400 --> 14:17.220
It is a stunning, almost unbelievable narrative cliffhanger.

14:18.000 --> 14:18.460
It's crazy.

14:18.680 --> 14:22.640
And Fleikert contrasts this directly with the structure we just analyzed in Job.

14:22.860 --> 14:24.160
Remember the sequence in Job?

14:24.160 --> 14:24.600
Yeah.

14:24.600 --> 14:30.160
It goes catastrophe, then survivor, then escape, then testimony.

14:30.460 --> 14:30.880
Exactly.

14:31.520 --> 14:34.180
The transmission of information is successfully completed.

14:34.580 --> 14:38.180
But look at the sequence in Mark as it stands in these early manuscripts.

14:38.620 --> 14:39.740
It's completely different.

14:40.000 --> 14:44.460
It goes discovery of the empty tomb, then the witnesses receive a message to deliver,

14:44.880 --> 14:48.080
then they escape, and then absolute silence.

14:48.300 --> 14:48.580
Nothing.

14:48.920 --> 14:49.180
Nothing.

14:49.180 --> 14:52.660
The narrative function of escape is completely inverted here.

14:53.100 --> 14:56.580
Instead of guaranteeing transmission, the escape interrupts it.

14:57.040 --> 15:02.180
It's like the video game NPC spawned, got right up to your character, the dialogue box opened,

15:02.420 --> 15:05.020
and then they just stood there, completely silent.

15:05.480 --> 15:07.340
That's a perfect way to visualize it.

15:07.620 --> 15:10.880
As a reader, the narrative machinery just feels fundamentally broken.

15:11.440 --> 15:14.140
And Flyxion makes a crucial methodological point here.

15:14.140 --> 15:17.160
This is a literary reading of the text as it stands.

15:17.820 --> 15:17.840
Right.

15:17.900 --> 15:19.340
We're looking at the text itself.

15:19.660 --> 15:19.780
Right.

15:19.900 --> 15:23.760
It is not making a historical claim about what actually happened on Easter morning,

15:23.920 --> 15:28.200
nor is it making a psychological claim about what the original author of Mark intended.

15:28.720 --> 15:30.540
Maybe they just lost the last page.

15:30.980 --> 15:31.320
Exactly.

15:31.600 --> 15:34.960
Maybe the original final page was physically lost in antiquity.

15:35.100 --> 15:38.180
Or maybe Mark meant to end it there, to be provocative,

15:38.540 --> 15:41.320
to force the reader to take up the mantle of spreading the message.

15:41.660 --> 15:42.680
We don't know the origin.

15:43.040 --> 15:43.380
Right.

15:43.380 --> 15:48.060
But structurally, purely mechanically, the transmission is left unnarrated.

15:48.480 --> 15:49.820
The machine is missing a gear.

15:50.280 --> 15:54.420
The reader is left to silently supply the propagation of the message in their own mind.

15:54.780 --> 15:58.780
And what's wild is that later, ancient readers clearly couldn't handle that.

15:58.840 --> 15:59.540
They really couldn't.

15:59.720 --> 16:01.240
As a reader, you feel that gap.

16:01.340 --> 16:04.460
It's a psychological itch you just desperately have to scratch.

16:04.840 --> 16:10.440
And what's amazing is how the paper shows that later writers actively repaired this broken machinery.

16:10.720 --> 16:11.000
Yes.

16:11.000 --> 16:14.240
Matthew and Luke came along a little later, and they fixed the gap.

16:14.680 --> 16:19.840
In Matthew's version, the women run to tell the disciples, and Jesus actually meets them on the way to confirm it.

16:20.260 --> 16:25.740
In Luke, the women report to the 11 disciples, though the disciples think it's an idle tale at first.

16:26.380 --> 16:28.940
And then you have the so-called longer ending of Mark.

16:28.940 --> 16:29.400
Right.

16:29.500 --> 16:30.600
Verses 9 through 20.

16:30.820 --> 16:31.220
Exactly.

16:31.360 --> 16:39.960
Which were tacked on to later manuscripts by scribes, which explicitly supplies the appearances of Jesus and the active proclamation of the message by the disciples.

16:39.960 --> 16:47.200
What's fascinating here is how it demonstrates that narratives can evolve and organize themselves without a single master designer.

16:47.200 --> 16:49.000
That just kind of patch themselves.

16:49.160 --> 16:49.320
Yeah.

16:49.460 --> 16:52.340
The later texts supply what Mark left unnarrated.

16:52.580 --> 16:56.240
They did sort of repair a felt gap, a structural tension.

16:56.560 --> 17:01.480
This is a brilliant example of a system modifying its own structure based on a constraint.

17:01.720 --> 17:01.900
Wow.

17:02.160 --> 17:06.860
The difficult, abrupt ending of Mark 16.8 changed the literary environment.

17:07.040 --> 17:11.380
It created a contradiction that provoked later authors to harmonize and adapt.

17:11.380 --> 17:12.820
So the story evolved.

17:13.300 --> 17:27.560
The narrative tradition generated an organized, completed transmission, not because one original author wrote it that way from a master blueprint, but because a community of later writers engaged in a blind process of retention and repair over decades.

17:28.020 --> 17:33.340
It's like the story itself demanded to be fixed in order to survive in the minds of the early church.

17:33.760 --> 17:34.280
Exactly.

17:34.720 --> 17:37.800
The machine repaired itself through different authors over time.

17:37.800 --> 17:42.000
But this brings up another massive issue in these ancient texts.

17:42.540 --> 17:43.200
Oh, definitely.

17:43.520 --> 17:44.080
Bookkeeping.

17:44.680 --> 17:45.420
Yeah, bookkeeping.

17:46.220 --> 17:59.720
If stories are these complex machines that transfer information over time and they are constantly being patched together by different people, how do they keep track of who's who, especially when things get super chaotic in the plot?

18:00.500 --> 18:05.460
That leads perfectly into the paper's next exploration, the mechanics of identity.

18:05.800 --> 18:06.720
This was fascinating.

18:06.720 --> 18:11.880
How is identity established in a narrative as opposed to just being assumed by the audience?

18:12.540 --> 18:21.980
Flyxion does a deep dive into the Mark and Passion narrative, the chaotic events leading up to the crucifixion, to show how the text actively manages this bookkeeping of identity.

18:22.420 --> 18:22.820
Right.

18:23.160 --> 18:27.880
Let me ask you, what is the most famous act of identification in the Bible?

18:28.540 --> 18:30.120
Oh, Judas's kiss, right?

18:30.140 --> 18:30.380
Yes.

18:30.380 --> 18:35.900
Mark 1444, Judas tells the armed crowd, the one I kiss is the man.

18:36.260 --> 18:38.140
Arrest him and lead him away under guard.

18:38.860 --> 18:39.260
Exactly.

18:39.700 --> 18:42.080
But wait, why does he actually need to do that?

18:42.360 --> 18:45.760
I mean, Jesus was a famous public figure teaching in the temple every day.

18:46.540 --> 18:47.900
Didn't they know what he looked like?

18:47.900 --> 18:51.080
That is the exact right question to ask.

18:51.380 --> 18:53.100
Historically, it might seem redundant.

18:53.620 --> 18:56.360
But narratively, why is it required?

18:57.180 --> 18:57.500
Why?

18:58.020 --> 18:59.080
Because it's night.

18:59.320 --> 18:59.900
It's dark.

19:00.040 --> 19:00.580
It's chaotic.

19:00.840 --> 19:03.140
There's an armed crowd and people are panicked.

19:03.560 --> 19:09.700
From a storytelling perspective, when a scene descends into chaos, identity becomes incredibly fragile.

19:09.700 --> 19:10.780
Oh, I see.

19:11.400 --> 19:18.800
The text has to use explicit tokens or physical actions to firmly attach an identity to a body so the reader doesn't get lost.

19:19.040 --> 19:22.780
They need an agreed upon explicit identification procedure.

19:23.100 --> 19:24.280
That makes total sense.

19:24.920 --> 19:28.420
It anchors the reader in the chaos, so you know exactly who is who.

19:29.040 --> 19:34.900
But Flyxion points out an even stranger example of these identity mechanics just a few verses later in Mark.

19:35.240 --> 19:36.420
Right, the naked guy.

19:36.420 --> 19:44.120
Right after Jesus is arrested and the disciples scatter, there is a young man, the Greek word is nianiskos, who is following Jesus.

19:44.640 --> 19:44.940
Okay.

19:45.380 --> 19:48.200
He's wearing nothing but a linen cloth over his naked body.

19:48.640 --> 19:55.120
The crowd seizes him, but he wriggles out of the linen cloth, leaves it in their hands, and flees away naked into the night.

19:55.280 --> 19:57.700
Which is just a bizarre detail.

19:58.380 --> 19:58.860
It is.

19:58.860 --> 20:09.940
And then, just two chapters later, at the empty tomb we just talked about, the women encounter a young man, again, the exact same word, nianiskos, sitting inside the tomb in a white robe.

20:10.260 --> 20:16.520
Now, I know some biblical scholars bend over backwards trying to say these two young men are the exact same person, right?

20:16.720 --> 20:18.900
Like it's a symbolic baptism or something.

20:18.900 --> 20:26.100
Yes, theologians and interpreters have spent centuries trying to connect them or identify who this young man was historically.

20:26.860 --> 20:31.840
But Flyxion's argument does not depend on identifying who this young man actually is.

20:32.060 --> 20:33.500
It doesn't matter who he is.

20:33.640 --> 20:33.960
No.

20:34.460 --> 20:36.740
Instead, it points out the structural mechanic at play.

20:37.280 --> 20:39.160
Look at the pattern of the first young man in the garden.

20:39.160 --> 20:44.360
You have seizure by the crowd, then a retained garment left behind, then an escaped person.

20:45.060 --> 20:50.600
The pursuers capture the identifying exterior, the cloth, but they completely fail to capture the actual person.

20:51.560 --> 20:54.500
Flyxion explicitly connects this to a much older story.

20:55.020 --> 20:59.820
Genesis 39.12, where Joseph flees from Potiphar's wife, leaving his garment in her hand.

20:59.980 --> 21:00.580
Oh, wow.

21:00.740 --> 21:02.640
It's the exact same structural motif.

21:03.000 --> 21:05.540
The pursuer gets the token, but loses the identity.

21:05.740 --> 21:07.540
It's a classic literary trope.

21:08.460 --> 21:08.940
Precisely.

21:09.160 --> 21:14.880
And to prove that this is a universal, mechanical constraint of storytelling rather than just a biblical quirk,

21:15.540 --> 21:18.840
Flyxion brings in a phenomenal comparison from early cinema.

21:19.300 --> 21:21.700
Have you ever seen any of Buster Keaton's silent films?

21:22.280 --> 21:24.720
Wait, the silent movie actor from the 1920s?

21:24.860 --> 21:25.180
Yes.

21:25.580 --> 21:27.680
What does he have to do with the Gospel of Mark?

21:28.160 --> 21:32.760
Flyxion specifically references a 1921 Buster Keaton short film called The Goat.

21:32.980 --> 21:33.260
Okay.

21:33.520 --> 21:36.940
In this film, Keaton's character wanders into a photographer's studio.

21:36.940 --> 21:40.560
He stands behind a mannequin of a tough guy while someone takes a picture.

21:40.940 --> 21:42.560
Oh, I think I've seen a clip of this.

21:43.560 --> 21:52.700
Through a series of highly farcical events, that manipulated photo gets slapped onto a wanted poster for a dangerous violent criminal called Deadshot Dan.

21:52.940 --> 21:53.200
Right.

21:53.200 --> 22:01.380
So you have a physical object, an exterior token of identity, that firmly attaches the wrong identity to an innocent man.

22:01.500 --> 22:02.920
And chaos ensues.

22:03.160 --> 22:03.660
Exactly.

22:03.660 --> 22:10.640
The entire plot of the film, all the physical comedy, turns on this misidentification generated by a physical object.

22:10.840 --> 22:12.020
That is wild.

22:12.640 --> 22:21.520
So Buster Keaton's The Goat and Judas' Kiss are essentially solving the exact same design problem in narrative, just from opposite sides.

22:21.520 --> 22:22.900
That's the brilliance of it.

22:22.900 --> 22:27.120
In Keaton's film, the token attaches the wrong identity to create comedy.

22:27.600 --> 22:34.280
In the case of Judas, the token, the kiss, is used to ensure the right identity is attached to create tragedy.

22:34.600 --> 22:34.720
Right.

22:35.060 --> 22:40.660
In both cases, identity is carried by an exterior mark that can be separated from the actual person.

22:41.360 --> 22:44.920
Flyxion points out that this kind of ambiguity isn't just a modern comedic trope.

22:45.060 --> 22:47.980
It has deep historical and theological resonance.

22:47.980 --> 22:55.700
And just to be completely clear for everyone listening right now, we are stepping into some deeply sensitive theological territory here.

22:55.940 --> 23:00.780
But we are looking at this purely through the lens of literary mechanics, not theology.

23:01.180 --> 23:01.620
Absolutely.

23:01.920 --> 23:04.860
We aren't taking sides on what actually happened historically.

23:05.120 --> 23:07.500
We are just looking at the source material provided.

23:08.020 --> 23:09.600
Yes, that is a vital distinction.

23:09.600 --> 23:19.640
Flyxion introduces a fascinating example of how this literary ambiguity played out in history by pointing to the Islamic tradition found in Quran 4, 1, 5, 7.

23:19.760 --> 23:19.940
Okay.

23:20.200 --> 23:22.600
This is the Shubiha Lahum tradition.

23:23.400 --> 23:29.520
This passage states that Jesus was not killed or crucified, but that it was, quote, made to appear so to them.

23:29.660 --> 23:29.900
Right.

23:29.980 --> 23:33.020
The idea of a substitute or an illusion on the cross.

23:33.020 --> 23:33.540
Exactly.

23:34.260 --> 23:45.220
Flyxion uses this to show that uncertainty around the crucifixion, the idea that a misidentification occurred in the chaos of the arrest, became a live interpretive option in the history of Muslim thought.

23:45.460 --> 23:45.700
Right.

23:45.820 --> 23:47.680
It was a possibility people considered.

23:48.080 --> 23:51.120
The point is not about whether that theology is true or false.

23:51.620 --> 23:53.900
The point is strictly about the mechanics of the narrative.

23:53.900 --> 24:04.180
The fact that this interpretation could even exist proves that darkness, crowds, and physical tokens in a chaotic scene are sufficient to generate real, lasting ambiguity for an audience.

24:04.940 --> 24:07.660
A narrative is partly a device for managing bookkeeping.

24:08.200 --> 24:10.520
Who is where and how do we know it's them?

24:11.040 --> 24:11.340
Yes.

24:11.660 --> 24:13.480
Here's where it gets really interesting.

24:13.480 --> 24:25.300
When you see how these tropes, the fleeing naked man, the retained garment, the identifying kiss, are used, you realize you are looking at the literal gears and levers of narrative construction.

24:26.120 --> 24:33.300
And that realization creates a massive methodological problem for historians, which brings us to the next core argument of the paper.

24:33.680 --> 24:33.960
Right.

24:33.960 --> 24:43.280
If we acknowledge that these ancient texts are heavily utilizing standard literary tropes and structural machinery, how do we know what is actually historical?

24:43.840 --> 24:47.800
We have to separate literary construction from historical existence.

24:48.060 --> 24:48.580
Right.

24:48.680 --> 24:52.720
So let me play devil's advocate here, because this is where a lot of people get tripped up.

24:52.920 --> 24:53.420
Go for it.

24:53.420 --> 25:07.980
If a story about a historical figure uses old recycled tropes, like having them do things that Joshua or Elijah did in the Old Testament, or having them born under a special star, does that automatically mean the person never existed?

25:08.460 --> 25:11.480
Does the presence of mythic structure erase the history?

25:11.820 --> 25:13.320
That is the crucial question.

25:13.320 --> 25:21.080
And to answer it, Flyxion discusses the work of Richard Carrier, specifically his Bayesian reference class arguments regarding the historicity of Jesus.

25:21.440 --> 25:21.660
Wait.

25:22.060 --> 25:22.500
Hold on.

25:22.500 --> 25:24.840
Bayesian reference class arguments?

25:25.520 --> 25:28.080
Treat me like I'm in middle school for a second.

25:28.280 --> 25:29.220
What does that actually mean?

25:29.780 --> 25:30.320
Fair enough.

25:30.520 --> 25:33.640
Imagine you find a new animal, and you want to guess if it lays eggs.

25:34.020 --> 25:35.060
You look at its traits.

25:35.580 --> 25:37.000
Maybe it has feathers and a beak.

25:37.460 --> 25:40.760
You put it in a reference class, or a bucket, labeled birds.

25:41.040 --> 25:41.340
Okay.

25:41.500 --> 25:42.420
I've got my bird bucket.

25:42.540 --> 25:52.180
Because you know most things in the bird's bucket lay eggs, you can use probability, Bayesian statistics, to confidently guess this new animal lays eggs, too.

25:52.500 --> 25:53.200
That makes sense.

25:53.260 --> 25:58.920
Richard Carrier attempts to use this kind of probability math to argue that Jesus was originally a mythic figure.

25:59.180 --> 26:05.560
He puts Jesus in a reference class bucket of other mythologized, dying, and rising gods from antiquity.

26:06.060 --> 26:06.420
Okay.

26:06.420 --> 26:12.640
And he argues that because most figures in that bucket didn't exist historically, Jesus probably didn't either.

26:13.140 --> 26:13.560
Okay.

26:13.640 --> 26:17.720
That makes sense as a concept, but I imagine that's highly debated.

26:18.240 --> 26:19.680
Heavily disputed.

26:19.680 --> 26:28.760
Flyxion explicitly notes that Carrier's reference class construction and his specific numerical conclusions are intensely contested by mainstream historians.

26:29.160 --> 26:31.560
The paper does not rely on Carrier's math.

26:31.900 --> 26:32.200
Good to know.

26:32.580 --> 26:37.000
What it does rely on is the underlying conceptual question Carrier raises.

26:37.640 --> 26:40.960
How do we deal with historical figures wrapped in mythic tropes?

26:41.420 --> 26:45.660
And to answer your devil's advocate question, does mythic structure erase history?

26:45.660 --> 26:47.160
The answer is no.

26:47.800 --> 26:48.340
It doesn't.

26:48.500 --> 26:51.420
Flyxion uses a phenomenal analogy here to explain why.

26:51.940 --> 26:52.720
Consider Superman.

26:53.000 --> 26:54.060
The comic book hero?

26:54.360 --> 26:54.660
Yes.

26:55.660 --> 27:04.740
Recognizing biblical or classical heroic structures in Superman, the fact that he's sent from the heavens by his father, raised by humble Earth parents, has miraculous powers,

27:05.300 --> 27:10.660
tells us everything about how Superman was constructed as a literary character by his creators in the 1930s.

27:11.460 --> 27:11.740
Right.

27:11.740 --> 27:14.780
It's a huge blend of Moses and Hercules and all that.

27:15.180 --> 27:24.680
It tells us about the cultural tropes they were drawing on, but finding those tropes in Superman does not give us independent attestation of whether a guy named Clark Kent actually exists in reality.

27:24.920 --> 27:27.560
Because Superman is purely a literary creation.

27:27.860 --> 27:28.100
Right.

27:28.520 --> 27:32.120
But now, conversely, let's look at ancient biographies of Alexander the Great.

27:32.360 --> 27:32.620
Okay.

27:32.620 --> 27:41.940
Those stories are absolutely packed with Heraklian motifs, tropes meant to compare Alexander to the mythical Hercules, slaying beasts and achieving impossible labors.

27:42.280 --> 27:42.380
Sure.

27:42.720 --> 27:56.080
But finding mythic structure in Alexander's story doesn't erase the massive amount of independent historical evidence, coins, inscriptions, contemporary accounts, that prove Alexander the Great was a real historical conqueror.

27:56.080 --> 28:04.320
That is such a clarifying way to look at it. Literary construction and historical existence are two completely separate variables.

28:04.820 --> 28:05.740
Entirely separate.

28:06.020 --> 28:12.300
Just because a story is organized beautifully with tropes doesn't mean it's purely fiction. But it also doesn't guarantee it's pure history.

28:12.560 --> 28:19.140
Exactly. So the big takeaway here when evaluating any ancient history is to ask yourself a very specific question.

28:19.840 --> 28:24.780
What historical residue remains once the literary construction has been subtracted?

28:24.780 --> 28:25.980
You have to peel it all back.

28:26.520 --> 28:36.740
You have to peel back the gears of the narrative machine, the borrowed tropes, the required constraints like the lone survivor and Job, and see what, if anything, is left underneath.

28:37.080 --> 28:38.720
And what do you usually find?

28:39.080 --> 28:48.160
Well, once you subtract the material that is fully explicable by the structural needs of storytelling, the historical residue left over might be large, like with Alexander.

28:48.520 --> 28:51.860
It might be small, or it might be totally empty, like with Superman.

28:51.860 --> 28:57.280
It's a much more rigorous method than just pointing at a parallel and saying, aha, it's fake.

28:57.420 --> 28:58.500
It's much more nuanced.

28:58.920 --> 29:02.600
Okay, I buy that humans can accidentally evolve a story over centuries.

29:03.220 --> 29:09.000
We're meaning-making creatures, you know, we fix the gaps in stories, we use tropes to make sense of chaos.

29:09.420 --> 29:11.860
But what happens if we remove humans entirely?

29:12.760 --> 29:14.120
Oh, here we go.

29:14.120 --> 29:18.200
Can a completely mindless system build a structure that looks planned?

29:19.400 --> 29:26.280
Let's strip away human biology, strip away human culture, and look at the cold, hard vacuum of space.

29:27.020 --> 29:36.720
This is where the paper makes a massive pivot and tests its thesis on the most impersonal, mathematically rigid system available, gravitational dynamics in the early universe.

29:36.900 --> 29:38.200
This blew my mind.

29:38.200 --> 29:43.240
Gravity provides the absolute cleanest case of organization without a prior plan.

29:43.800 --> 29:45.920
The governing law of gravity is brutally simple.

29:46.280 --> 29:47.240
Mass attracts mass.

29:47.680 --> 29:47.800
Right.

29:48.360 --> 29:51.980
The initial conditions of the early universe can be specified almost exactly.

29:52.440 --> 29:57.100
And crucially, there is absolutely nothing in the law of gravity that refers to the final outcome.

29:57.540 --> 29:57.840
Right.

29:58.000 --> 30:02.240
Gravity's equation doesn't have a variable for a spiral galaxy or solar system.

30:02.760 --> 30:04.540
It doesn't even know what a planet is.

30:04.540 --> 30:05.100
Exactly.

30:05.700 --> 30:08.700
So how does the incredible structure of the universe form?

30:09.660 --> 30:16.920
It starts shortly after the Big Bang with a nearly uniform distribution of matter, a vast, incredibly hot soup of particles.

30:17.120 --> 30:18.220
Just as smooth soup.

30:18.780 --> 30:21.020
But nearly uniform is the key word.

30:21.640 --> 30:30.820
There are microscopic density perturbations, tiny, random quantum fluctuations that make some microscopic areas just fractionally denser than their surroundings.

30:31.220 --> 30:32.800
Okay, so it's a little lumpy.

30:32.800 --> 30:34.260
Just a tiny bit lumpy.

30:34.540 --> 30:37.600
Gravity acts blindly on those perturbations.

30:38.300 --> 30:43.620
A slightly denser region exerts a slightly stronger gravitational pull, so it attracts a little more matter.

30:44.080 --> 30:45.300
Which makes it even denser.

30:45.860 --> 30:48.640
Because it attracts more matter, it becomes even denser.

30:49.160 --> 30:55.500
This stronger gravity draws in even more material from its surroundings, which means the neighboring regions become depleted.

30:55.500 --> 31:03.780
And the result of this blind, compounding process over billions of years is what astrophysicists call the cosmic web.

31:04.000 --> 31:04.580
It's beautiful.

31:04.580 --> 31:11.600
If you've ever seen a computer simulation of the universe on a macro scale, it is mind-blowing.

31:11.600 --> 31:14.520
It looks like glowing neurons in a brain.

31:14.820 --> 31:15.380
It really does.

31:15.740 --> 31:20.880
You have massive filaments of dark matter in galaxies stretching for millions of light years.

31:20.880 --> 31:23.160
You have vast, flat sheets.

31:23.160 --> 31:28.060
You have dense nodes where the filaments intersect, which are super clusters of galaxies.

31:28.420 --> 31:28.600
Mm-hmm.

31:28.960 --> 31:32.700
And in between, you have huge, seemingly empty voids.

31:33.200 --> 31:37.340
Though Flyxion is careful to note that voids aren't truly empty.

31:37.820 --> 31:42.240
They are just massively under-dense regions that still contain some scattered galaxies.

31:42.240 --> 31:42.720
Right.

31:42.840 --> 31:44.240
They aren't completely empty space.

31:44.640 --> 31:50.120
But the point is, this incredible, highly organized web wasn't planned.

31:50.640 --> 31:53.240
It is the complementary consequence of redistribution.

31:53.740 --> 31:56.940
Concentration in one area causes depletion in another.

31:57.480 --> 32:02.300
When we were prepping for this, I was trying to visualize how this works without a plan.

32:02.820 --> 32:05.140
And I thought of a snowball rolling down a hill.

32:05.420 --> 32:06.960
It's a good starting analogy.

32:07.300 --> 32:08.160
Walk me through it.

32:08.160 --> 32:13.980
Okay, if you roll a snowball down a slightly bumpy hill, the snow doesn't know it wants

32:13.980 --> 32:16.540
to become a massive, perfectly spherical boulder.

32:16.900 --> 32:17.920
It has no blueprint.

32:18.060 --> 32:18.680
It just rolls.

32:19.560 --> 32:24.740
And as it rolls, it accumulates snow based on the immediate, local bumps it happens to

32:24.740 --> 32:25.000
hit.

32:25.780 --> 32:26.860
But here's the trick.

32:27.400 --> 32:31.880
Every bit of snow it accumulates changes its shape, which changes its center of gravity,

32:32.060 --> 32:34.560
which changes how it interacts with the next bump on the hill.

32:35.160 --> 32:35.620
Oh, nice.

32:35.620 --> 32:39.180
The snowball alters its own future path, just by rolling.

32:39.860 --> 32:41.920
That captures the feedback loop perfectly.

32:42.320 --> 32:44.720
But with gravity, we have to take it one step further.

32:45.080 --> 32:45.780
Okay, how so?

32:46.020 --> 32:49.640
With your snowball, the bumpy hill is already there, waiting to be rolled over.

32:50.060 --> 32:55.180
In the early universe, the snowball rolling actually warps the hill itself.

32:55.480 --> 32:56.060
Wait, what?

32:56.060 --> 33:02.960
The accumulation of matter changes the gravitational field, which changes the literal shape of space-time

33:02.960 --> 33:06.020
around it, determining where the next bit of matter will fall.

33:06.760 --> 33:07.160
Wow.

33:07.720 --> 33:10.360
It builds the hill while it rolls down it.

33:10.620 --> 33:11.200
Precisely.

33:11.200 --> 33:16.700
And we do have to add a necessary scientific qualification here, which Flaconian includes.

33:17.240 --> 33:21.940
Gravity alone, acting on a perfectly symmetric state of matter with zero angular momentum,

33:22.300 --> 33:27.040
wouldn't create those beautiful spinning spiral galaxies we see in telescope images.

33:27.040 --> 33:27.600
Right.

33:27.760 --> 33:28.420
They have to spin.

33:29.180 --> 33:33.520
The standard astrophysical account is that galaxies acquire angular momentum, that spinning

33:33.520 --> 33:35.320
motion, through tidal torques.

33:35.520 --> 33:40.660
As these irregular clumps of matter grow, they exert uneven gravitational pulls, torques,

33:40.800 --> 33:42.300
on each other, which imparts a spin.

33:42.660 --> 33:44.260
So they yank on each other.

33:44.580 --> 33:46.800
This requires initial asymmetry.

33:47.220 --> 33:52.720
So a galaxy's final shape depends on velocity, dispersion, gas dissipation, all these complex

33:52.720 --> 33:53.440
fluid dynamics.

33:53.440 --> 33:55.960
But none of that weakens the conceptual point.

33:56.640 --> 34:03.120
The point is that a simple local law applied to slightly uneven initial conditions generates

34:03.120 --> 34:08.520
massive, complex organization without a single byte of that organization being represented

34:08.520 --> 34:09.040
beforehand.

34:09.860 --> 34:14.060
And if we connect this to the bigger picture, it yields the first general principle of Flaconian

34:14.060 --> 34:14.520
synthesis.

34:15.180 --> 34:19.700
Principle one, structure does not entail a prior representation of the structure.

34:19.920 --> 34:20.700
You don't need a plan.

34:20.700 --> 34:25.660
You can have a fully organized system at the end without any blueprint existing at the

34:25.660 --> 34:25.940
beginning.

34:26.560 --> 34:26.580
Okay.

34:26.760 --> 34:29.880
So gravity builds the massive macro structure of the universe.

34:30.220 --> 34:33.160
It builds the cosmic web, the filaments, the galaxies.

34:33.780 --> 34:36.400
But what happens when we zoom in?

34:36.680 --> 34:37.440
Down to the planets.

34:37.640 --> 34:37.820
Right.

34:38.000 --> 34:40.320
What happens on the planets that form within that web?

34:40.800 --> 34:42.900
Because rocks don't just sit there, do they?

34:43.160 --> 34:44.040
They actually evolve.

34:44.480 --> 34:45.720
They absolutely do.

34:45.720 --> 34:50.680
Flyxion draws heavily here on the fascinating work of Robert Hazen and his colleagues, who

34:50.680 --> 34:53.480
proposed the framework of mineral evolution.

34:54.260 --> 34:55.020
Mineral evolution?

34:55.520 --> 34:55.780
Sorry.

34:55.780 --> 34:56.180
Like biology?

34:56.720 --> 35:02.280
The core idea is that a planet does not have a fixed, static inventory of minerals from the

35:02.280 --> 35:02.860
day it forms.

35:02.980 --> 35:04.440
It's not a set menu.

35:04.680 --> 35:04.840
Okay.

35:05.100 --> 35:09.400
The mineral inventory evolves over time through distinct sequential stages.

35:10.060 --> 35:12.900
Hazen outlines 10 stages of mineral evolution.

35:13.160 --> 35:13.760
10 stages.

35:13.760 --> 35:19.940
It starts with roughly a dozen basic minerals present in the preplanetary nebula, stuff like

35:19.940 --> 35:21.200
diamonds and olivine.

35:21.600 --> 35:22.140
Just a dozen.

35:22.520 --> 35:23.040
Just a dozen.

35:23.520 --> 35:29.760
Then you get planetary accretion, melting, differentiation into a core and crust, plate

35:29.760 --> 35:34.460
tectonics introducing water, and eventually the massive influence of biological life, pumping

35:34.460 --> 35:35.680
oxygen into the atmosphere.

35:36.060 --> 35:36.320
Wow.

35:36.680 --> 35:41.100
Through these stages, those original dozen minerals combine and recombine.

35:41.100 --> 35:45.200
Today, Earth has over 5,000 recognized mineral species.

35:45.440 --> 35:46.060
5,000?

35:46.560 --> 35:47.200
From a dozen?

35:47.780 --> 35:50.280
That is a massive explosion of chemical complexity.

35:50.840 --> 35:54.320
And what's crucial is that this isn't just a list getting longer.

35:54.480 --> 35:56.100
It's a scaffolding process.

35:56.360 --> 35:56.940
That's the key.

35:57.360 --> 36:02.220
The set of minerals present at one epoch is a direct consequence of the planet's earlier

36:02.220 --> 36:02.700
history.

36:02.900 --> 36:07.320
And that new set of minerals, in turn, conditions the chemistry that is possible later.

36:07.540 --> 36:08.540
It changes the rules.

36:08.540 --> 36:12.280
The planet's history modifies its own chemical possibility space.

36:12.840 --> 36:16.180
Every new rock creates a new environment for a different rock to form.

36:16.500 --> 36:20.980
And this has massive implications for the origins of life, or what we call prebiotic chemistry.

36:21.320 --> 36:23.740
Let's dive deep into that prebiotic chemistry.

36:24.180 --> 36:27.180
Because minerals act like a scaffold for life.

36:27.180 --> 36:32.780
I was reading this part of the paper, and it points out laboratory work showing that the

36:32.780 --> 36:39.380
surfaces of common minerals, like oxides, silicates, and carbonates, can actually select and concentrate

36:39.380 --> 36:44.380
specific amino acids and sugars from a chaotic, watery chemical soup.

36:44.740 --> 36:46.560
Yes, the surfaces are highly active.

36:46.940 --> 36:50.880
Clay surfaces can actually scaffold the formation of RNA oligomers.

36:50.880 --> 36:56.500
The atomic structure of the clay literally lines up the building blocks of life so they can

36:56.500 --> 36:57.080
bond together.

36:57.660 --> 37:00.440
The rock is doing the organizing work.

37:01.080 --> 37:05.500
Now, Flyxion carefully grades the certainty of this, that minerals adsorb and concentrate

37:05.500 --> 37:09.300
molecules is an established, undeniable fact in chemistry laboratories.

37:09.540 --> 37:09.760
Right.

37:09.840 --> 37:10.700
We know that happens.

37:10.700 --> 37:16.520
That this process is entirely sufficient for the origin of life on its own is graded as

37:16.520 --> 37:21.540
a plausible mechanism, because real geochemical environments on the early Earth were vastly

37:21.540 --> 37:23.700
messier than clean laboratory experiments.

37:24.100 --> 37:25.620
It wasn't a sterile beaker.

37:25.840 --> 37:26.200
Exactly.

37:26.680 --> 37:30.880
But to help us visualize how this might have worked in reality, the paper offers a vivid

37:30.880 --> 37:34.460
illustrative analogy, the membraneless planetary cell.

37:34.680 --> 37:35.980
I love this concept.

37:36.520 --> 37:37.540
Paint that picture for us.

37:37.540 --> 37:43.280
It is not saying the early Earth was literally a giant living cell with regulated transport.

37:43.700 --> 37:47.760
What it describes is a distributed planetary-scale chemical reactor.

37:48.360 --> 37:48.380
Okay.

37:48.980 --> 37:50.220
Picture the early Earth.

37:50.540 --> 37:52.220
Imagine the Hadean Earth.

37:52.660 --> 37:54.840
It's violent, it's hot, it's barren.

37:55.340 --> 37:56.280
But look at the rocks.

37:56.820 --> 38:02.440
Millions of microscopic cracks and pores in the pumice and volcanic rock provide isolated,

38:02.640 --> 38:03.560
enclosed spaces.

38:03.760 --> 38:05.320
Like tiny little test tubes.

38:05.580 --> 38:05.920
Yes.

38:05.920 --> 38:10.360
The mineral surfaces inside those pores provide the catalytic scaffolding.

38:10.900 --> 38:14.200
The movement of geothermal water transports chemicals in and out.

38:14.740 --> 38:20.280
And natural wet-dry cycles from geysers or tides repeatedly concentrate and disperse the materials.

38:20.480 --> 38:20.660
Wow.

38:20.980 --> 38:26.780
You have geological boundaries acting as compartments long before biological lipid membranes ever evolved.

38:27.120 --> 38:31.820
And speaking of those wet-dry cycles, we have to talk about Richard Leith's fast moon theory.

38:31.820 --> 38:35.640
Because it is incredibly cool, even if it's debated.

38:36.140 --> 38:37.620
Oh, this is a wild theory.

38:37.960 --> 38:40.060
Leith proposed a theory called tidal forcing.

38:40.660 --> 38:44.940
The idea is that billions of years ago, the moon was much, much closer to the Earth.

38:45.480 --> 38:48.580
Because it was closer, its gravitational pull was immense.

38:49.080 --> 38:50.680
And the Earth was spinning much faster.

38:50.680 --> 38:51.760
Very fast.

38:52.280 --> 38:55.120
We are talking about a day lasting maybe four to six hours.

38:55.800 --> 39:06.300
This would mean the coastal areas were subjected to massive, violent tides, walls of water hundreds of feet high, flooding inland and completely drying out every two to six hours.

39:06.300 --> 39:10.120
It would have been a profoundly violent environment, yes.

39:10.520 --> 39:13.300
But chemically, an incredibly productive one.

39:13.920 --> 39:25.100
Leith suggests that these rapid, extreme cycles of dilution from the flood and concentration from the evaporation would act almost exactly like a giant, natural, polymerase chain reaction machine.

39:25.900 --> 39:26.820
A PCR machine.

39:27.240 --> 39:30.740
That's what scientists use in labs to rapidly multiply DNA, right?

39:31.180 --> 39:34.240
Heating it up to split the strands, cooling it down to copy them?

39:34.660 --> 39:35.220
Exactly.

39:35.220 --> 39:44.340
These constant, rapid tidal cycles, driven by the orbit of the moon, could favor the replication and multiplication of nucleic acid-like polymers.

39:44.660 --> 39:51.260
The entire coastline of the planet acting as a giant DNA multiplying machine driven by the orbit of a massive moon.

39:51.840 --> 39:53.340
That is such a cinematic image.

39:53.500 --> 39:54.220
It's brilliant.

39:54.760 --> 39:59.600
Now, as you said, the expert consensus on this specific moon theory is that it's contested.

39:59.600 --> 40:07.160
But as a mechanism, it is highly plausible and shows exactly how planetary dynamics can blindly drive chemical organization.

40:07.760 --> 40:08.000
Yes.

40:08.000 --> 40:10.940
But this brings up a mathematical problem.

40:11.500 --> 40:20.740
If the origin of life requires a very specific, rare chemical event to happen, like RNA forming by chance, aren't the odds just astronomically low?

40:20.740 --> 40:28.860
The paper addresses this direct criticism with a look at probability math, specifically equation 1, which explores the mathematics of rare events.

40:29.260 --> 40:29.760
Okay.

40:29.760 --> 40:36.460
The equation is P greater than or equal to 1 equals 1 minus 1 minus P to the N.

40:37.320 --> 40:37.600
Okay.

40:37.740 --> 40:38.100
Hold on.

40:38.340 --> 40:39.920
Math on a deep dive.

40:40.200 --> 40:41.160
Let's translate that.

40:41.160 --> 40:59.280
In plain English, if you have a chemically interesting event that has a very, very tiny probability of happening, let's call that probability P, and you assume each trial is independent, the chance of it happening at least once approaches absolute certainty if the number of trials, N, is large enough.

40:59.440 --> 40:59.640
Right.

40:59.920 --> 41:05.640
So if I roll a billion-sided die once, I'm almost certainly not going to hit the number 7.

41:05.920 --> 41:06.560
Very unlikely.

41:06.560 --> 41:19.200
But if I roll that billion-sided die a trillion times a second for a billion years across every single crack and pore on the entire Earth's surface, I am definitely going to hit a 7 eventually.

41:19.460 --> 41:20.220
That's the logic.

41:20.680 --> 41:35.960
Standard probability tells us that an event that would be fantastically unlikely in one specific rock pore during one specific tidal cycle can become virtually guaranteed when planetary geology supplies a near infinite number of trials over geological time frames.

41:35.960 --> 41:39.540
So time and space just overwhelm the low odds.

41:39.940 --> 41:43.820
A small probability does not exclude an event if N is large enough.

41:44.160 --> 41:51.140
But, and this is a massive but, the paper points out that this standard probability equation eventually breaks down.

41:51.900 --> 41:52.220
Why?

41:52.680 --> 41:55.060
Because the system stops playing by the rules.

41:55.880 --> 41:57.540
The system starts cheating.

41:58.000 --> 41:59.700
Yes, and this is where the magic happens.

41:59.960 --> 42:04.640
This is the transition from random chemistry to self-facilitating chemistry.

42:04.640 --> 42:13.900
The independence assumption in that probability equation, the idea that every die roll is totally separate from the last one, fails exactly where the interest in chemistry begins.

42:14.540 --> 42:15.140
Why does it fail?

42:15.500 --> 42:23.780
Once a chemical network appears that accidentally improves the conditions for its own continuation, later trials are no longer drawn from the original probability distribution.

42:24.420 --> 42:25.740
The dice become loaded.

42:25.740 --> 42:26.920
Precisely.

42:27.560 --> 42:35.100
Phleoxion introduces the formal theory of autocatalytic sets, developed by researchers like Stuart Kaufman, Wim Hordick, and Mike Steele.

42:35.920 --> 42:36.960
Autocatalytic sets.

42:37.520 --> 42:50.700
In a formal autocatalytic set, you have a network of chemical reactions where every single reaction is catalyzed, meaning sped up or facilitated, by a member of that very same set, drawing from a basic food set of available background molecules.

42:50.700 --> 42:53.680
So it's a closed loop of chemical cooperation.

42:54.280 --> 42:54.760
Exactly.

42:55.240 --> 42:57.860
But the paper introduces a unique synthesis here.

42:58.360 --> 43:02.380
It focuses on something called environment-mediated autocatalysis.

43:03.000 --> 43:03.940
Whoa, slow down.

43:04.020 --> 43:05.540
What does that actually mean in plain English?

43:06.080 --> 43:10.580
I'm glad you stopped me because it is a profound distinction that changes how you view chemistry.

43:11.460 --> 43:13.360
Imagine a loop of reactions.

43:13.880 --> 43:19.060
Chemical A turns into B, B turns into C, C turns into D, and D turns back into A.

43:19.060 --> 43:20.780
Okay, a nice, neat circle.

43:21.660 --> 43:27.960
In a standard autocatalytic set, maybe molecule D directly interacts with molecule A to copy it, like a primitive gene.

43:28.460 --> 43:32.080
But in Flax's model, D doesn't have to directly copy anything.

43:32.620 --> 43:33.480
What does it do then?

43:34.120 --> 43:38.960
Maybe molecule D just slightly alters the local pH of the water in that specific rock pore.

43:39.100 --> 43:41.840
Or maybe D slightly modifies the surface of the clay.

43:41.840 --> 43:48.880
And that new pH, or that new clay surface, happens to make the reaction of A turning into B happen 10 times faster.

43:49.420 --> 43:50.320
Oh, I see.

43:50.780 --> 43:56.140
The feedback operates through the shared environment, not just through direct molecule-to-molecule contact.

43:56.780 --> 43:57.240
Exactly.

43:57.900 --> 44:00.760
None of the molecules know they are in a loop.

44:01.020 --> 44:05.080
No single molecule is acting like a modern gene intentionally copying itself.

44:05.220 --> 44:08.200
But the causal path is closed and positive.

44:08.620 --> 44:10.440
The environment helps them survive.

44:10.440 --> 44:14.180
The presence of the network changes the environment to favor the network.

44:14.700 --> 44:20.800
This is a much weaker, much easier requirement for nature to stumble upon than a fully formed replicating gene.

44:21.200 --> 44:26.040
And a distributed planetary reactor of millions of rock pores is perfectly suited to find it.

44:26.440 --> 44:27.900
So what does this all mean?

44:28.500 --> 44:29.260
Is this evolution?

44:29.920 --> 44:30.840
Is this life?

44:31.100 --> 44:33.380
Have we just described the origin of life?

44:33.380 --> 44:37.260
Flyxion is incredibly strict with the terminology here.

44:37.560 --> 44:38.740
The answer is no.

44:38.940 --> 44:40.880
This is not yet Darwinian evolution.

44:41.400 --> 44:44.760
And we shouldn't necessarily call it life yet.

44:44.980 --> 44:45.440
Oh, really?

44:45.940 --> 44:47.260
This is chemical selection.

44:47.720 --> 44:49.320
It is differential persistence.

44:49.920 --> 44:52.420
You have networks that facilitate their own continuation.

44:52.920 --> 44:54.400
So they persist and accumulate.

44:55.060 --> 44:58.320
Networks that don't do this are simply washed away by the next tide.

44:58.320 --> 45:00.700
It's just survival of the stickiest.

45:00.700 --> 45:03.620
It is persistence before reproduction.

45:04.440 --> 45:11.220
Full Darwinian evolution requires reproduction with heritable variation and differential reproductive success.

45:11.220 --> 45:15.220
So we don't need to invoke a magical spark of life.

45:15.820 --> 45:24.380
We just have random chemistry, which finds persistent chemistry, which becomes self-facilitating chemistry, which leads to environmental modification.

45:24.840 --> 45:30.780
And only much, much later do you get cell boundaries, heredity, and true Darwinian evolution.

45:31.300 --> 45:31.700
Correct.

45:32.160 --> 45:35.320
The conceptual transition is incredibly gradual.

45:35.660 --> 45:40.100
The most important conceptual threshold is passed long before genes even exist.

45:40.220 --> 45:41.200
When is that?

45:41.660 --> 45:46.580
It is passed the moment a chemical system acquires a primitive historical dependence,

45:46.980 --> 45:51.700
when its products alter the probability distribution governing its subsequent reactions.

45:52.840 --> 45:54.820
And this gives us principle two of the paper.

45:55.400 --> 45:58.740
Once outcomes modify the conditions that produce later outcomes,

45:59.220 --> 46:03.740
history ceases to sample repeatedly from an unchanged possibility space.

46:04.220 --> 46:06.620
The rules of the game change while the game is being played.

46:06.800 --> 46:08.380
It's phenomenal to think about.

46:08.380 --> 46:13.020
But if simple, blind chemistry can change its own environment to ensure its survival,

46:13.740 --> 46:17.720
what happens when actual, complex living organisms start doing it?

46:18.160 --> 46:20.040
Because that brings us to biology.

46:20.560 --> 46:22.780
And then, inevitably, back to human culture.

46:22.780 --> 46:25.460
We move up the ladder of complexity here.

46:26.020 --> 46:30.620
In evolutionary biology, there is a very well-established concept called niche construction.

46:31.020 --> 46:31.580
Niche construction.

46:31.580 --> 46:36.960
This is the phenomenon where organisms don't just passively adapt to a static environment.

46:36.960 --> 46:40.220
They actively modify their environment.

46:40.680 --> 46:45.360
And by doing so, they alter the natural selection pressures that will act on their own offspring.

46:45.360 --> 46:49.120
The classic example is a beaver building a dam, right?

46:49.280 --> 46:50.260
Perfect example, yes.

46:50.360 --> 46:55.460
The beaver cuts down trees and floods a valley, changing a fast-flowing river into a still pond.

46:56.020 --> 47:02.420
Now, the beaver's offspring have to evolve to survive in a pond ecosystem, not a river ecosystem.

47:03.220 --> 47:06.900
The organism literally rewrote the rules for its own future evolution.

47:06.900 --> 47:09.000
That is the exact perfect example.

47:09.600 --> 47:13.040
And Phlegon maps this exact same feedback loop onto human culture.

47:13.400 --> 47:17.760
Successful stories, like the narrative constraints we saw in the Book of Job or the Gospel of Mark,

47:18.100 --> 47:19.660
change the cultural environment.

47:20.180 --> 47:20.560
How so?

47:21.160 --> 47:27.320
Once the Book of Job establishes the lone survivor motif as an incredibly powerful way to convey tragedy,

47:27.760 --> 47:29.960
that structure becomes available for reuse.

47:30.180 --> 47:32.360
It changes what audiences expect.

47:32.780 --> 47:33.060
Right.

47:33.060 --> 47:38.060
When Mark 16.8 leaves a jarring, terrifying silence at the end of the Gospel,

47:38.460 --> 47:43.440
it creates a theological and narrative tension that forces later authors, like Matthew and Luke,

47:43.560 --> 47:49.700
to adapt and create new, harmonized endings to survive in that newly established cultural constraint.

47:50.400 --> 47:56.540
The cultural artifacts construct a niche that forces future cultural artifacts to evolve in a specific direction.

47:56.960 --> 48:00.520
It's the exact same math as the beaver dam, just applied to ideas.

48:01.080 --> 48:01.620
It is.

48:01.620 --> 48:04.740
But studying this historically is tricky, isn't it?

48:05.280 --> 48:10.120
How do we trace that evolution without falling back into the trap of assuming a mastermind author?

48:10.920 --> 48:11.980
That is the danger.

48:12.240 --> 48:18.180
And it's why Phlegon brings in a methodological check from philosophy, specifically Nietzschean genealogy.

48:18.800 --> 48:19.060
Nietzsche.

48:19.640 --> 48:19.960
Okay.

48:20.460 --> 48:28.120
Friedrich Nietzsche was absolutely adamant about refusing to infer the origin of a value from the self-description of the finished value.

48:28.440 --> 48:29.000
What does that mean?

48:29.000 --> 48:34.400
A moral concept today might claim it is the product of pure reason or divine revelation.

48:35.020 --> 48:43.380
But Nietzsche argues it actually arose through messy historical struggle, social reinforcement, and constant agonizing reinterpretation.

48:43.660 --> 48:49.900
The paper specifically points to Nietzsche's second essay on the genealogy of morality, dealing with the concept of punishment.

48:50.380 --> 48:51.320
Walk me through that.

48:51.320 --> 48:57.000
Nietzsche argues that the origin of a practice and its current purpose are almost entirely distinct.

48:57.580 --> 49:00.160
Take the practice of imprisoning someone as punishment.

49:00.700 --> 49:00.860
Okay.

49:01.440 --> 49:05.540
Today, if you ask society, the purpose of prison is rehabilitation or deterrence.

49:05.700 --> 49:09.360
But that was almost certainly not its original purpose centuries ago.

49:09.360 --> 49:17.920
A practice that survives over a long time is constantly being hijacked, reinterpreted, and redirected by new powers to serve new ends.

49:18.520 --> 49:19.620
It just mutates.

49:20.100 --> 49:23.580
Meanings accrete over time, like layers of paint on an old house.

49:24.140 --> 49:32.740
Therefore, you cannot look at a modern prison, listen to its current justification, and assume that this justification was the blueprint present at its origin.

49:32.740 --> 49:38.400
So, we have to adopt genealogy as a universal method for looking at organized systems.

49:39.060 --> 49:41.200
The central prohibition of the paper is this.

49:41.740 --> 49:44.860
Do not project the finished structure backward into its origin.

49:45.260 --> 49:47.060
This raises an important question.

49:47.300 --> 49:51.100
How do we stop projecting finished structures backward into their origins?

49:51.760 --> 49:52.040
Right.

49:52.280 --> 49:55.140
A mature galaxy does not imply a cosmic blueprint.

49:56.020 --> 50:00.700
An autocatalytic chemical lube does not require the universe to have anticipated life.

50:00.700 --> 50:10.220
And a highly organized, brilliant narrative tradition does not, by itself, imply a single originating author with a master plan.

50:10.660 --> 50:11.160
Exactly.

50:11.740 --> 50:17.680
And that prohibition clears the ground for Falection's grand synthesis, bringing all these incredibly disparate threads,

50:18.120 --> 50:25.680
biblical literature, deep space, planetary rocks, beaver dams, and Nietzsche, together into one unified theory of organization.

50:25.900 --> 50:28.720
How do you even put all of that into one unified theory?

50:29.040 --> 50:30.240
What's the common denominator?

50:30.240 --> 50:38.440
Flyxion offers a final synthesis, a formula of required ingredients to generate an organized historical structure without a prior plan.

50:38.900 --> 50:45.720
You need variation, plus constraint, plus parallelism, plus differential persistence, plus feedback, plus time.

50:46.280 --> 50:46.920
Six things.

50:47.260 --> 50:51.560
If you have those six things, you will inevitably generate complex organization.

50:52.020 --> 50:53.180
Okay, I want to test this.

50:53.480 --> 50:56.000
Let's see if this actually maps onto the domains we talked about.

50:56.000 --> 50:59.620
Let's take gravity and the cosmic web.

51:00.020 --> 51:00.860
What's the variation?

51:01.500 --> 51:05.600
The variation is the initial quantum density perturbations, the little lumps.

51:05.600 --> 51:09.340
Okay, the constraint is the brutal law of physics, gravity.

51:10.020 --> 51:16.680
And the feedback, which is the crucial part, is that the redistribution of matter alters later gravitational relations.

51:17.380 --> 51:19.820
It builds the hill while it rolls down it.

51:20.300 --> 51:20.780
Exactly.

51:21.360 --> 51:22.140
What about rocks?

51:22.540 --> 51:23.300
Mineral evolution.

51:24.000 --> 51:26.900
The variation is the massive range of chemical possibilities.

51:26.900 --> 51:30.540
The constraint is thermodynamics and heat.

51:31.120 --> 51:37.320
And the feedback is that new minerals literally create new reaction environments for future chemistry to happen on.

51:37.740 --> 51:38.100
Spot on.

51:38.520 --> 51:40.260
It works for autocatalysis, too.

51:40.740 --> 51:42.860
Variation is random molecular encounters.

51:43.440 --> 51:45.200
Constraint is reaction kinetics.

51:46.060 --> 51:51.800
Feedback is the chemical network altering its own local environment, like the pH or the clay surface.

51:52.280 --> 51:52.600
Yes.

51:52.600 --> 51:53.480
And culture.

51:54.180 --> 51:57.420
Variation is variant versions of a story told around a fire.

51:58.360 --> 52:00.180
Constraint is audience retention.

52:00.560 --> 52:01.980
If they get bored, the story dies.

52:02.740 --> 52:09.400
And the feedback is successful stories forcing later authors to address newly established cultural norms, like fixing the ending of Mark.

52:09.900 --> 52:12.220
It is a stunningly unified framework.

52:13.180 --> 52:16.900
And mathematically, it all hinges on how transitions occur in a system.

52:17.860 --> 52:20.060
Flags and Contrast two types of models.

52:20.840 --> 52:21.140
Okay.

52:21.140 --> 52:24.220
First, you have a fixed transition model.

52:25.060 --> 52:26.420
Imagine a mathematical equation.

52:26.900 --> 52:31.820
The state of a system at time t plus 1 is a function of the state at time t.

52:32.260 --> 52:39.520
The state changes, the dice roll, but the rule governing the change, the dice themselves, stays exactly the same forever.

52:40.020 --> 52:40.760
So it's rigid.

52:41.280 --> 52:45.440
A system like this, no matter how long it runs, cannot construct its own distribution.

52:45.960 --> 52:48.380
It is forever trapped by its initial rules.

52:48.380 --> 52:51.160
But that's not how our universe actually works, is it?

52:51.400 --> 52:52.300
